{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":1326806,"num_turns":38,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"a1c6ca01-be82-48e1-8b8f-daf37ce9641f","total_cost_usd":5.888455,"usage":{"input_tokens":662918,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":102841,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":663361,"outputTokens":102866,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":5.888455,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Done. **16/16 workloads pass, bit-exact** (`max_abs = 0.000e+00`, `max_rel = 0.000e+00`), with `solution.json` and `kernel.py` on disk.\n\n## What I did\n\nFused the reference's data-movement layer, leaving its GEMMs untouched:\n\n1. **`_permute_qkv`** — replaces `.transpose(1,2).contiguous().view(...)` for the query path.\n2. **`_gqa_sum_permute2`** — collapses the K and V paths from **6 kernels + 4 full-size temporaries** into **one launch with zero temporaries**, fusing GQA un-repeat (`view.sum(dim=2)`) with the transpose and reshape, and streaming both tensors in one pass.\n3. **`addmm`** for `grad_enc`, folding the accumulate into the second GEMM's epilogue instead of a separate read-modify-write pass.\n\nBoth kernels index flat over the *output* so each program writes a fully contiguous run; that beat the tiled formulation by ~20% (permute 108→86 µs, GQA 181→113 µs), and I swept `(BLK, num_warps)` across every workload shape.\n\n## The finding that shaped the result\n\nThe reference is **96.4% GEMM time**, and those GEMMs already run at hipBLASLt's fp32 ceiling — 94 TF/s, identical to a pure square GEMM, with rocBLAS 29% slower. The tolerance then rules out touching them. I measured this rather than assuming it:\n\n- True fp32 vs exact `float64` math matches only **28.8%** of elements against the required 99% — the tolerance is tight enough that it effectively demands hipBLASLt's *specific reduction order*, not merely correct arithmetic.\n- bf16x3 split-precision emulation (the usual way to beat fp32 on CDNA): **0.05%** match.\n- Merging the two weight-gradient GEMMs into one: **7%**. Merging the two `grad_enc` GEMMs: **18%**.\n\nEach of those is a plausible-looking optimization that would have failed scoring, and the last two are only detectable by checking against the tolerance — they're bit-inexact but *look* fine at `1e-6`. I also tried overlapping the KV permute on a second stream; it was a **2.6% regression**, since the GEMMs already saturate the CUs, so I rejected it.\n\n## Honest assessment of the speed\n\nEnd-to-end this is **1.02–1.11×** (~1.03× aggregate), roughly **55% of the analytic SOL bound**. That gap is almost entirely the vendor fp32 GEMM: at 94 of ~157 TF/s peak, the six GEMMs alone set a floor at ~28.1 ms of my 29.1 ms. I removed ~3.6% of overhead down to near bandwidth, but I could not honestly claim more without either reordering the GEMMs or using lower precision — both of which I verified would fail correctness. Given \"a wrong kernel scores nothing,\" I kept the numerics exact.\n\nI used 3 of 5 attempts. Also verified: cold JIT start (2.3 s) and non-contiguous inputs, both exact, since re-scoring happens on a different GPU from a clean tree.","ttft_ms":3629,"ttft_stream_ms":3629,"time_to_request_ms":66,"type":"result","duration_ms":1452139,"uuid":"edfb0269-9765-4284-b03a-2192655f9b45"}
